EXT. CORN MAZE - NIGHT

Walls of dry stalks twice head-height. Moon behind thin cloud. The paths twist like intestines. Wind rustles the husks.

WYATT (20s, letterman jacket, flashlight) leads, sweeping the beam. JOLENE (20s, hoodie, nervous giggle) clutches his sleeve. BUBBA (30s, big man, camo cap, a bag of beef jerky) bumps behind them. TINA (teen, braces, a phone held up like a candle) trails at the rear.

They stop at a fork. Wyatt aims the beam left, then right. Corn stalks lean in on both sides like crowding spectators.

WYATT
Which way?

TINA
I'm out of battery.

JOLENE
We came in from the left.

BUBBA
We came in from the right.

They freeze. The wind stops. Somewhere in the rows, a stalk snaps. Another. A third, closer. Wyatt kills the light.

A shape rises from the left path. Tall. Thin. Wearing a feed-sack head. It tilts. Stares.

Jolene screams. Bubba drops the jerky. Wyatt grabs both girls and wheels right. They sprint, crashing through stalks, but the shape follows without hurrying, gliding between the rows like a fish. Tina trips. Bubba hauls her up by the hood, and the four of them burst into a small clearing at the center of the maze.

A wooden scarecrow, upright on a pole, waits there. Its sack head turns to face them.

They scatter.
